Notebook[{
Cell["Terminal Velocity of Coffee Filters", "Title"],
Cell["Christoph Ortenburg\n28 September 2026", "Subtitle"],
Cell["Abstract", "Section"],
Cell["Coffee filters were dropped in stacks and tracked on video so the drag could be written as F_drag = b v^n. Both n and b are read from the terminal speed, the constant speed a stack holds once drag cancels its weight. On a distance-time graph that speed is the slope of the straight middle of the fall. For four filters, three trials gave 2.571 m/s, 2.635 m/s, and 2.535 m/s. Each one is within 2.2% of their average, 2.580 m/s. A log-log plot of terminal speed against mass for the seven recorded stacks has slope 0.5676, so n = 1.76. The intercept of that line gives b = 7.40 x 10^(-3) in SI units. The exponent sits closer to quadratic drag than to linear drag.", "Text"],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Finding the straight section", "Section"],
Cell["A falling stack does not hold one speed for the whole drop, so the distance-time graph is not one straight line. Three features separate the parts of the trace.\n\nAt the start the curve bends upward. The slope is shallow just after release and gets steeper while the filters are still speeding up. Drag has not yet caught up with the weight, so that curved opening is not terminal velocity.\n\nThe middle is the part I kept. There the points lie on a straight line: equal time steps cover nearly equal distances, and a running slope stops climbing and sits near 2.5 m/s. A straightedge on that stretch does not show a bend.\n\nThe end of the trace is the floor. Near 5.9 m the position stops rising, because the filters have landed. Just before that flat ending the trace steepens once more, which is not a new constant speed. Both of those end features were left out of the fit.\n\nOn trial 1 the straight middle runs from 0.866 s to 1.888 s. The shaded band on the graph is that interval. The same three features fixed the interval on the other trials.", "Text"],
